The current `manifolding-keyboard' state, spelled out in full. Leads row 1
because it is the answer to "what will my next key do?" — the only question a
modal editor makes you ask constantly.

** Segment
The text is rebuilt on every recompose, not cached. That is deliberate: the
value is a `symbol-name' of a buffer-local variable, which is the cheapest
read in the bar, and caching it would need invalidating on a hook the
keyboard does not otherwise have. The keyboard calls
`manifolding-modeline--state-changed' from `manifolding-keyboard-set-state',
which recomposes the bar when — and only when — the state actually changed.
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(manifolding-modeline-define-segment keyboard-state
  :doc "Current manifolding-keyboard state, in full."
  :face nil
  :side :left
  ;; A lambda, not `'(fn)'. A quoted call would have to be `eval'd at build
  ;; time, and a lambda is byte-compilable and needs no eval at all.
  :construct (lambda () (manifolding-keyboard-modeline-state-name)))
#+end_src

** Why a rebuild and not a redraw
The composer bakes each segment's text into `mode-line-format' as a literal
string, which is what keeps redisplay free of segment work. The cost is that
a segment whose text changes has to be rebuilt, not merely redrawn. That is
the right trade for every segment here — the expensive ones (branch, atlas,
clock, doctor) change on the order of seconds, and the cheap one (this) is
rebuilt on the order of a keyboard state change. Nothing in the bar changes
often enough for the distinction to matter, and everything in it is too
expensive to compute per redisplay.
